@maxamillion The issue is that I do not have control over a password a user chooses. If the user is selecting a password that is completely or partly matching a parameter that a module is returning, the module will not be usable any more. An error would result in the same issue as now. It would not be predictable.

We have roles in ansible-freeipa that are using lots of modules. I am not sure that a role or module could compare a sensitive user password to all the returned parameter names to fail early before even doing changes.

In my opinion it is the responsibility of a module to make sure that no sensitive information is leaked into the returned parameters. I do not see a way how to address this in Ansible without introducing issues like https://github.com/ansible/ansible/issues/70455. Ansible does simply not know if there is an overlap of sensitive and insensitive information only because a user has chosen a password that accidentally matches the name or parts of the name of a parameter.
